    "updated_at": "2026-10-08T10:06:42Z",
    "body": "<details><summary>AI Review</summary>\r\n\r\nThis looks right to me: SRI's parse metadata now returns an (ordered) set of maps, map equality is by value, and Infra's \"unordered equal\" is subset+superset, which also tolerates the duplicates SRI can currently produce (it uses list append rather than set append — probably worth an SRI issue).\r\n\r\nA few things:\r\n\r\n- Nit: the \"; or\" is on the last list item; it should move to the first one now that you're rewriting both lines.\r\n- Switching to \"is empty\" also means a consumer whose integrity contains only unknown algorithms (e.g. md5-…) now matches any preload. That's consistent with SRI's \"empty set matches\" semantics, but worth calling out and testing.\r\n- Since SRI no longer stores options at all, is the XXX about issue #116 still accurate as written?\r\n- Have we considered comparing \"get the strongest metadata from set\" of each side instead? That would allow reusing a sha384-B preload for a sha256-A sha384-B consumer with identical security.",
